#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221181 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9aeac73-8ee3-5fef-a47c-a96e3545d89c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Linear Approximations Near Equilibria"}] :question/id #uuid "87b69a53-94f5-51e6-9c3a-e403abbf1fa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21674" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4x(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast=[2, \\ 0]^T?$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/d4/d422a2e5dc28d7f3163879aa16f212b3573e9f2eb20f6aa1dd28ce2e8b55466b.svg) and \n![](images/2d/2df6f3b3a0c55d34c93291a33fca3f3f33f61a7491beb4cc495540a0f5ab5ae6.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/a5/a59de210f69e55b9de475da491dc1de9f32ccad7253ab1e4ab70714b96210e6d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/28/2832518b9ff9a0795b8b1f337a92fbd97b8b1628da52ee88a2a9c88d95c330bc.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/0e/0e9e15cf4c6eb8e07e42b3c32fffe458c230511e50e02650f8c9bf4226801b5a.svg) to get\n\n![](images/cf/cf167eaf02d0500310e31db9b3db546c3dcd1b395438bdf5daaeefec2af500ae.svg)\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n![](images/39/39b57ee5a860cfd3b73c64bebf93a77a193339530ae6970f931972a2e345a1df.svg)\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n![](images/3c/3c80deae8395322a7abb6e106ccf084428b93e00e9b8087f53aa0c8bb76c00e4.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221182 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "0f4ae74c-67d0-5c0e-99db-e834253b3851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21675" :question/problem "Given the system of differential equations, determine whether the system is almost linear at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast= [0, \\: 0]^T.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/d8/d80eb6b2beaebedf071ab77f752156bfd4b433f3762deefc008751fbf0fd24f4.svg) and \n![](images/a8/a8e250dcd16fe545e136b5b58697f8ebf9ee674c44d31bebd5d4e9b67eb61a96.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/5f/5f841f13bd8e85cbac3b84dc57a3e9edaeef97ddedfa271e6befba2c97131015.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/77/77748e3e4d751bc1e9f6f2f8979440e46dd820e0263b893993ee8fd1bcdae50c.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/9b/9b03c0103e4d9b884776d63daa94a28cf078ec8e84bdaaf183744b6d4a493d75.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)-(0)(0) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{almost linear}}$ at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "777fae40-2377-5e63-89f6-5c857beba2b2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying an Isolated Critical Point of a Nonlinear System"}] :question/id #uuid "fd2d2d61-53a6-51c1-811c-41393270ddba" :question/math-academy-id "e-21676" :question/problem "![](images/86/8635a08be7b8fa85e73b8683e0f17b95f9c834116d9ac29267d2ee5e98002856.svg)\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system’s solutions near the equilibrium ![](images/dc/dc2fe4cdba6d5eab5c763479aa564b60cf7c5c4736d958e7341223c30a580aab.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n![](images/f8/f876ff09a7b8c0bfd16d6597389967749eeade8305ada19fe4770d575705c601.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f6/f60e24b209fce8d306850ca088ebbc091fb9ddd11cda74e46ac7e2dc9cc61610.svg) and \n![](images/57/572bf982c2dfc04ad3b0771d666e9956b5e316e9148574c53788fc66ca9cfd35.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of ![](images/41/41df3ddfc93bfc6b75f58565e44fde8667b575eab70d34a6ad26f0fc7d7b9f00.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/ea/eacac468e4ce8b3ea07d93ddfb7f6336d8a3f6a822bedaffa07cd5431aa31a1d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n![](images/8a/8aac1cd3e1f495a9f509442a8599882e61092618f03e43431d68b7e807d65c69.svg)\n\nThus, we have:\n\n![](images/83/83cdeef9e17081a8236ac8d14ea9d8b8fda3da046d95f84fadbbf93d72a9bc0a.svg)\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/81/81720438d92024448a479c3488e0478f5de9a4b26c59544fa738ca2cf6c1e6d7.svg) to get\n\n![](images/1b/1b42df3f7cf42a954e20ade84aeb884235bf693c1c03258ed316f4b13cbc01e6.svg)\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ![](images/00/009ee734f37f8813ce80ea9e89d4156cb498825273cf0f21e52818218ea99434.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n![](images/d4/d4a328abcc87b1a35120504bb31e047476aa18af01cef1498964d2a4b777f201.svg)\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues\n![](images/74/74dd38ba634c0a0d0150d29c4bc21320be4372679610877f8dab85b4dedc9182.svg)\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an ![](images/92/921a21d96fcb849ac36e1369ed9798e1c895e2549aa9e3643da1198d8403d556.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "518db6f0-044f-53a1-b4ac-02b876a8ce5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444458} {:answer/id #uuid "75de21b8-e810-56ba-aa15-d9778b4ddb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444470} {:answer/id #uuid "8408a1d3-b7f2-5609-8b87-660697d4c0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444473} {:answer/id #uuid "900d00fb-6d70-57d8-b664-39cb2b3658bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "900d00fb-6d70-57d8-b664-39cb2b3658bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444476} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e0bda42-6407-5b29-9afc-20a9efb70a23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "551b9ac3-2040-5138-8370-255e0e43ce5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444460} {:answer/id #uuid "82c7071e-b6e9-5a86-bb8e-b48b3bc6df5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82c7071e-b6e9-5a86-bb8e-b48b3bc6df5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d48c5f0-1607-5c9c-9667-b921f37fbbb8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1391978c-3f23-5276-a4d7-85ffc5372cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334933" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{x} & -2y \\\\ -2x & -{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a6634ee-910f-55f8-9d60-5c8c995940b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368110} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4f5267-b311-5097-b0ef-bfed5c3b88c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6634ee-910f-55f8-9d60-5c8c995940b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368110} :answer-field/id #uuid "decc9c2a-219a-5a8c-88e2-22756060d4c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4abcfb-223d-5381-849b-3fb4ca82f7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368113} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c1586e-ee5f-57ad-89b4-73c090ce68fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368114} {:answer/id #uuid "c830c193-7c1a-5c97-8afa-819b0b740a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368115} {:answer/id #uuid "032b476e-9204-5a08-8fd0-f04b6ce96683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b476e-9204-5a08-8fd0-f04b6ce96683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368116} :answer-field/id #uuid "734e541f-2137-5488-88d1-d745fa7b5050" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b642f63-61e2-5bfd-b745-caa14baed777" :question/math-academy-id "q-334949" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(1,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72340dc1-5dda-5425-8945-6d48e1143d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368118} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecdaede-59ca-5ab4-a488-3865e7146c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368119} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf12431-0a5a-53d0-8213-0b17195297a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368120} {:answer/id #uuid "aada5db9-0588-53a6-8255-685956e45ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecdaede-59ca-5ab4-a488-3865e7146c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368119} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c3dfe3-4874-5366-8605-975305c9e601" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "550f465e-0ee4-5206-960f-79ab7b2721a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368123} {:answer/id #uuid "b099dcf5-d989-5208-bec1-e4d448a6144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b099dcf5-d989-5208-bec1-e4d448a6144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368124} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e06f99-af82-5f1c-a3a7-0a32284d0555" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c191cb5-1a9c-5ce5-b394-bfa06fef9db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334954" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2(0)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9680fc-dfa9-5411-aeca-81659b7dec8a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Almost Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca75dc5-e0b6-5c02-874f-e33f31f85c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368127} {:answer/id #uuid "32a0ded4-8d51-595f-b54e-b33b7a306ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368128} {:answer/id #uuid "51d90af4-54c9-5128-ba35-9b467a03fa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368129} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2bef84-c1de-5272-bda5-a317f440d239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a0ded4-8d51-595f-b54e-b33b7a306ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :